<h2>How the question arose</h2>
<p>The corporate debtor’s resolution process had run its course: a resolution plan was approved by the Committee of Creditors and placed before the NCLT for confirmation under Section 31 of the Insolvency and Bankruptcy Code, 2016. While confirmation was pending, the CoC — unanimously — sought to withdraw the plan. Its reason was striking: under the resolution professional’s management, the corporate debtor’s opening balance had grown from roughly Rs. 30 crore to about Rs. 165 crore. Proceeding with the plan, the creditors argued, would hand the successful resolution applicant a windfall; a fresh Form G should issue and expressions of interest be invited afresh. The erstwhile promoter supported withdrawal and offered a one-time settlement. The successful resolution applicant resisted, standing on the binding character of the approved plan.</p>
<p>The NCLAT rejected the attempt to withdraw the approval and revived the pending application for confirmation of the plan. The financial creditors carried the matter to the Supreme Court.</p>

<h2>The holding: approval binds both sides</h2>
<p>The Court anchored its answer in the architecture of the Code. Section 12A permits withdrawal of an admitted insolvency application with the approval of ninety per cent of the CoC — but only up to a point. As the judgment puts it, withdrawal of an application <strong>"can only be till the CoC approves the plan."</strong> Once the plan is approved and placed before the Adjudicating Authority, neither the application nor the approved resolution plan can be pulled back.</p>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old"><h4>Before CoC approval</h4><p>The process is fluid: plans may be modified or improved within the CoC’s procedures, and the insolvency application itself may be withdrawn under Section 12A with a 90% CoC vote.</p></div>
  <div class="arrow">→</div>
  <div class="col new"><h4>After CoC approval</h4><p>The approved plan binds the successful resolution applicant and the CoC alike. It awaits only the Section 31 scrutiny of the Adjudicating Authority; withdrawal — by either side — is not contemplated by the Code.</p></div>
</div>
<p>The settled rule that a successful resolution applicant cannot walk out of its own approved plan was extended, in terms, to the creditors: the principle, the Court held, applies equally to the CoC, and the Code permits neither further modification nor withdrawal of an approved plan at the instance of either party. The continued existence of the CoC until implementation does not dilute the plan’s binding effect.</p>

<h2>The windfall argument</h2>
<p>What of the Rs. 165 crore sitting with the debtor? The Court did not treat the creditors’ concern as irrelevant — it relocated it. Improved profitability alone cannot justify rejecting a plan that otherwise satisfies Section 30(2); but the Adjudicating Authority, while deciding confirmation, is to examine the company’s current financial position and cash balance against the plan’s terms. The contentions of the successful resolution applicant and of the erstwhile promoter — including the settlement proposal — are to be considered by the NCLT when it takes up the confirmation application and the promoter’s application together, as expeditiously as possible. The Court was careful to say that its observations beyond the binding character of the plan were prima facie and would not govern the NCLT’s final adjudication.</p>

<h2>Practical significance</h2>
<p>For insolvency practice, the decision closes a door that lenders had occasionally tried to push open when market conditions or the debtor’s fortunes shifted between CoC approval and NCLT confirmation. Three consequences follow. First, the CoC’s commercial wisdom must be fully exercised before the vote: valuation disagreements, upside scenarios and the possibility of a promoter settlement are matters for negotiation before approval, not grounds for repentance after it. Second, resolution applicants gain real certainty — the bid they win is the bid they get, insulated from second thoughts on the creditor side. Third, where genuinely supervening circumstances arise, the forum is the Adjudicating Authority under Section 31, whose scrutiny remains confined to the statutory parameters.</p>
<div class="note">
<p>For creditors weighing a late settlement offer from a promoter, the sequencing is now decisive: a Section 12A withdrawal requires action before plan approval. Once the CoC has voted a plan through, the window has closed, and the matter rests with the NCLT within the four corners of the Code.</p>
</div>
